from mk import mk
mk("fxh2ccZKEb0","Create Evolving Cinematic Drones In Pigments - Full Tutorial",
 "random-rate-lfo-wt-drone-parallel-panned-filters-granular-layer",
 "Evolving cinematic drone: random-modulated LFO rates on WT position and two parallel, opposite-panned filters, plus a randomized granular sample layer and FX chain",
 "Build a one-key evolving cinematic drone where nothing repeats: a wavetable oscillator whose position is swept by a very slow free-running LFO whose RATE is itself nudged by a slow smooth random modulator (small amount, ~0.07), super-unison (~2.4 detune), 1 s positive-curve attack / ~4 s decay / lower sustain / 2 s release, filter 1 (resonance, keytrack, drive, filter envelope, tiny random LFO on cutoff) and a parallel 12 dB MS-20-style filter 2 driven by a second slow LFO with its own random rate modulation (~0.13), the two filters panned ~40% left/right; add a granular sample engine (triangle grains, randomized density/size/volume/pitch, LFO-scanned start position) and a touch of white noise into both filters; FX: shimmer → ping-pong tape echo (dotted 1/8) → gentle multiband compression on lows/highs, plus reverb → soft tape distortion → second reverb, mod wheel to drive.",
 ["Wavetable + sample/granular engines (or Web Audio PeriodicWave crossfades + AudioWorklet granular), 2 filters in parallel with pan, 2+ LFOs with free-run and random modulators routable to LFO rate","Shimmer reverb, tape/ping-pong delay, multiband compressor, reverb, tape distortion"],
 [("note_or_chord","midi","Single held note or chord (works low too)",True),("grain_sample","audio","Original/free granular-friendly sample",True)],
 [("evolving_drone","audio","Non-repeating cinematic drone")],
 ["Use an original or free wavetable and sample; factory table/sample names are not copied.","Values are presenter's display units; adjust by ear.","Keep random modulation of LFO rates small — large amounts make motion erratic."],
 [("Wavetable engine: pick a folded/rich table; route LFO1 (free-running, very slow) to wavetable position from a start around the table's middle.","wt","Slow timbre sweep"),
  ("Make LFO1's speed drift: route a slow, smooth, free-running random modulator to LFO1 rate with a small amount (~0.07) so the sweep speeds up and slows down unpredictably.","random→lfo-rate","Sweep rate audibly varies"),
  ("Size: super-unison with ~2.4 detune, main tune 0; no glide/legato.","unison","Big wide body"),
  ("Amp envelope: ~1 s attack with slightly positive curve, ~4 s decay, slightly lower sustain, ~2 s release. Second envelope similar for filter.","env","Slow swell and long tail"),
  ("Filter 1: lowered cutoff with filter envelope, some resonance, keytracking (higher keys open more) and a little drive; add LFO1 to cutoff with a tiny range.","filter-1","Unpredictable subtle filter motion"),
  ("LFO2: another slow free-running LFO whose rate is modulated by a second random source (~0.13). Add filter 2 as a 12 dB low-pass with character (MS-20-like), set filters to PARALLEL, send sources to both, and let LFO2 move filter 2 cutoff. Pan filter 1 ~40% left and filter 2 ~40% right.","filter-2","Two independently drifting filtered images L/R"),
  ("Engine 2 (granular): load an original granular-friendly sample into both filters; enable granular with random density both directions, random size, some random volume and pitch; change grain shape to triangle (smoother than the rhythmic default); scan the start position with LFO1.","granular","Mysterious, spooky moving grain layer"),
  ("Add a little white noise into both filters (no sub needed).","noise","Air/texture"),
  ("FX A: shimmer (bigger size) → tape echo ping-pong dotted 1/8 → multiband compression mostly taming lows (and some highs) while letting mids through. FX B: reverb (longer decay) → soft tape distortion → second reverb.","fx","Wide, spacious, mid-focused drone without low build-up"),
  ("Map mod wheel to distortion drive; trim low end/clipping as needed and save.","macro","Playable intensity control")],
 ["Holding one note for 30 s yields no audibly repeating cycle.","Left and right channels move differently (filters panned).","No low-end build-up after the multiband stage."],
 ["drone","wavetable","random-modulation","lfo-rate","parallel-filters","granular","shimmer","web-audio"])

mk("aNVjrDQANMw","Ep. 04 | How to Build Powerful Cinematic Drone Sounds (Modular Synth Sound Design)",
 "three-pillar-drone-wt-feedback-loop-granular-filter-before-shimmer",
 "Three-pillar cinematic drone (layering, movement, FX): wavetable VCO pair + self-feedback delay loop + granular layer, effects after filters with master low-cut as tension control",
 "Build a cinematic drone from three layers and three pillars: (1) a wavetable VCO pair (second one octave down on a slowly volume-modulated VCA) through a low-pass with slow LFO on table position; (2) a self-oscillating feedback loop from a granular/delay processor with no input (feedback raised until it sings, any re-patched input attenuated) through its own resonant low-pass; (3) a granular layer recording 2–3 random sources, with intensity, short grain length for lo-fi, quantized pitch randomization, follow/auto-scan, overdub, S&H choosing layers and an LFO on grain length — all summed through a master filter BEFORE a shimmer reverb so resonance in the low band excites the reverb, using the filter's low cut/cutoff as the scene's tension control (more highs = tension, more lows = suspense).",
 ["Wavetable oscillators, VCAs, LFOs, resonant low-pass filters","Delay/granular processor that can self-feedback, granular sampler with record/overdub","Shimmer reverb, delay (Web Audio: DelayNode feedback loops with gain < 1 plus limiter, implementation note)"],
 [("scene_mood","text","Desired tension/suspense balance",False),("grain_sources","audio","2–3 recorded sources (clicks, mic, random samples)",True)],
 [("cinematic_drone","audio","Layered, moving drone bed with tension control")],
 ["Never leave a feedback loop unattenuated — you lose control.","Presenter worked mono into stereo FX at the end; stereo grains are better if available.","Gear-specific modules are mapped to generic functions only."],
 [("Layer 1: two wavetable VCOs on separate VCA channels, VCO2 one octave below; slowly modulate VCO2's level so the sub appears sometimes; low-pass to cut buzzy harmonics.","wt-layer","Already a usable drone with reverb"),
  ("Movement on layer 1: very slow LFO on wavetable position/axis; optionally resonance/drive/wave-shaper for more harmonics.","lfo","Slowly morphing tone"),
  ("Layer 2 (feedback loop): with no input, raise the processor's feedback until it starts self-generating; keep delay lines close; run it into its own resonant low-pass; if feeding output back to input, attenuate it first and lower input gain.","feedback","Noisy, unpredictable tonal bed; sub-octave/tone controls shape it"),
  ("Layer 3 (granular): record 2–3 different short sources (even a click or mic sound) as layers; set intensity (grain count), short grain length for lo-fi, quantized pitch randomization; use follow mode to auto-scan through the recording, forward or reverse; overdub (e.g. whistling) for new tones.","granular","Three tonal strata: high ethereal, mid, sub"),
  ("Movement on layer 3: sample-and-hold selects which recorded layer plays; an LFO modulates grain length.","s&h","Ever-changing grain layer"),
  ("Sum layers, then a master low-pass with resonance BEFORE shimmer reverb and delay so resonant low harmonics feed the reverb.","master-filter","Harmonic correlation in the low band; shimmer adds high overtones from them"),
  ("Use the master filter's low cut / cutoff as the tension control: more high frequencies = tension ('something is coming'), lows = suspense; choose reverb type for mood; end a scene by pulling inputs out and leaving a little wet.","perform","Drone follows scene dynamics")],
 ["Muting each layer changes the bed distinctly (tone/noise/texture).","Raising master cutoff increases perceived tension; lowering it darkens without stopping.","Feedback layer stays bounded (no runaway) over a 1-minute hold."],
 ["drone","wavetable","feedback-loop","granular","shimmer","filter-before-reverb","tension","modular","web-audio"])

mk("0NXGraBGWvE","Create Ambient Drones in Ableton Live 12.2 (Hybrid Reverb Tutorial)",
 "single-macro-reverb-mix-deadzone-freeze-overdub-drone",
 "One-knob reverb → drone instrument: macro range 0–99 = wet/dry, 100–110 = muted dead zone, 110–127 = freeze + overdub drone with LFO speed, plus LFO-modulated EQ/width, unsynced auto-filter, limiter and randomizable algorithm",
 "Wrap an algorithmic reverb in a macro rack that doubles as an ambient drone generator: one 'drone' macro maps wet/dry over its lower range, a 100–110 dead zone that mutes the rack (utility gain/off) to avoid the loud boom when leaving freeze, and from 110 up engages freeze-with-overdub so new notes add into the held reverb while the same macro sets a jittered, shaped LFO from fastest (at 110) to ~0.1 Hz (at the top); that LFO modulates mid-EQ frequency (+30), mid gain (−25), low edge (+15), high edge (−20) and stereo width (+15); a parallel-moving auto filter (~1.5 kHz, 10% res, ~5% drive, its own time-mode LFO at 10%) stays out of sync; a limiter guards overdub build-up; other reverb parameters (algorithm, decay, size, per-algorithm params) are randomizable, EQ/pre-delay/drone excluded, and good results saved as variations.",
 ["Algorithmic reverb with freeze and overdub/'add' toggle (Web Audio: feedback delay network with feedback→1 and an input-gate for overdub, implementation note)","Macro/rack mapping with min/max ranges, LFO device, auto-filter, limiter, gain utility","Optional randomize-macros function"],
 [("notes","midi","Simple tones/chords (piano works) fed into the reverb",True)],
 [("drone_rack","audio","Reverb that becomes an evolving frozen drone instrument")],
 ["Overdub keeps adding energy — always keep a limiter at the end and play sparingly.","Exact macro ranges come from the presenter's mapping screens (some shown only on-screen); treat the numeric ranges here as starting points.","Long decays keep ringing when entering the dead zone — wait or bypass/re-enable the rack."],
 [("Build a rack: algorithmic reverb (convolution off) → LFO device → auto filter → limiter → utility (gain). Expose 16 macros: algorithm, decay, size, params 1–5 (map each algorithm's own 5 controls to the same macros), low/mid/high EQ, mid gain, vintage, width, pre-delay, drone.","rack","Macro rack controlling all reverb parameters"),
  ("Reverb EQ: band 1 and band 4 as steep (48 dB/oct) edge filters mapped to 'low EQ' and 'high EQ'; bands 2+3 frequencies to 'mid EQ', their gains mapped inversely to 'mid gain'.","eq","Tone shaping of the reverb only"),
  ("Map the 'drone' macro: dry/wet over 0–99; utility on/off+gain so 100–110 is silent; freeze ('infinite') and overdub ('add') switch on at ≥110; LFO on/off, rate (fast at 110 → ~0.1 Hz at max), jitter and wave shape (≈75%→20%) across 110–127; auto-filter on and its LFO time also on this range.","drone-macro","Turning past 110 freezes the reverb into a drone whose motion slows as the knob rises"),
  ("LFO device targets with depths: mid-EQ frequency 30, mid gain −25, low EQ 15, high EQ −20, width 15.","lfo","Drone tone and width breathe continuously"),
  ("Auto filter: frequency ~1500 Hz, resonance 10%, MS-type model, drive 5%, clip on; its own LFO in time mode at 10% amount, deliberately not synced to the rack LFO.","auto-filter","Filter motion never lines up with EQ/width motion"),
  ("Play: below 100 use as a normal reverb; above 110 play a note or two and let them overdub into the frozen space; adjust mid/high/low EQ in real time if boomy or trebly.","perform","Evolving atmosphere from a few notes"),
  ("Randomize the rack (exclude EQ, pre-delay and drone macros) to audition reverb spaces; save good results as rack variations and re-save the rack preset.","randomize","Fast discovery of new spaces/drones")],
 ["Macro at 105 produces silence; moving from 110 to 109 produces no loud boom.","At ≥110 new notes add to the held sound rather than replacing it.","Increasing the macro toward max audibly slows the drone's motion."],
 ["reverb-freeze","drone","macro","overdub","lfo","randomize","ambient","web-audio"])

mk("t0X_HQ25jmg","Pedals in Parallel - Discovering new ways to use effects you thought you knew",
 "parallel-fx-network-phase-flip-dry-cancel-insert-loop",
 "Parallel FX network: same input to up to three effects, heavy drive alongside clean delay, phase-flip a loop to cancel its dry and keep only wet, per-loop EQ/levels, effects inside the delay loop",
 "Turn a serial chain into a parallel effects network for synth/pad/lead layers: feed the same dry input to up to three effect loops and sum them (so a high-gain drive can growl in the background while a delay stays clean), use a per-loop phase invert to cancel the dry component of an effect without a wet-only mode (inverted dry + summed dry ≈ wet only), check for effects that already invert phase (chorus) against the dry, balance with per-loop EQ, send/return levels and master, and place an effect inside a delay's feedback/insert loop so repeats get processed — the portable parallel-distortion wet/dry recipe that was missing from the library.",
 ["Parallel mixer/bus with per-branch gain, phase invert and EQ (Web Audio: split to 3 GainNodes, sum; invert = gain −1; implementation note)","Drive/distortion, delay (ideally with insert/feedback loop), chorus/flanger, tremolo"],
 [("dry_input","audio","Synth/guitar/source signal",True)],
 [("parallel_mix","audio","Layered multi-texture effect sum")],
 ["About three parallel branches is the practical maximum before phase issues become problems (a branch's internal dry/wet mix counts as another signal).","Some effects invert phase internally; in parallel with dry they partially cancel — always check the phase switch per branch.","Source demo is guitar pedals; signal-flow concepts are generic."],
 [("Baseline: compare serial distortion→echo with echo→distortion to hear order-dependence.","serial","Two different serial textures"),
  ("Parallel: split the same dry input to drive and delay branches and sum. Keep drive high and sustaining in its branch while the delay branch handles clean repeats.","parallel","Growl under clean echoes — impossible in series"),
  ("Remove the dry from an effect that has no wet-only mode: flip the phase of that branch so its dry component cancels against the dry in the sum, leaving (mostly) the delay.","phase-invert","Wet-only repeats without a kill-dry switch"),
  ("Chorus = vibrato + dry: test phase flip on modulation effects; inverted dry won't fully 'unbake' into pure vibrato, and some pedals already invert phase, so pick the setting that sounds fuller.","phase-check","Avoid thin/cancelled modulation branches"),
  ("Balance each branch with its EQ, send and return levels and a master volume; take the dry out entirely when only effects are wanted.","balance","Clean, controllable parallel blend"),
  ("Insert loop: put another effect (e.g. modulation/space) inside the delay's insert/feedback path so delayed sound is processed again on each recirculation.","insert-loop","Repeats grow more spacious/distant each pass"),
  ("Swap in different trios of effects (e.g. delay + modulation + tremolo, envelope-controlled tremolo) to discover combinations.","explore","New hybrid textures")],
 ["With phase flip on a full-mix delay branch, the summed output has noticeably less dry level.","Drive branch level can be raised without the delay repeats distorting.","Adding a fourth branch increases comb/phase artifacts (audible thinning)."],
 ["parallel-processing","distortion","delay","phase","wet-dry","fx-routing","web-audio"])
